Take n cliques, each of n vertices, any two sharing at most one vertex, merged into one graph. Set aside a small reserve subset of the vertices whose required colors are already fixed by the shape of the rest of the graph before any coloring happens: the reserve is chosen so that its own completion is fully determined by, and encodes, whatever the greedy coloring of everything outside it turns out to need. Color every vertex outside the reserve one at a time, each one taking any of the n colors not already used by its already-colored neighbors, until only the reserve remains. Because the reserve's structure already encodes the exact shape the rest of the coloring will leave behind, completing it is not a separate step bolted on afterward, it is reading off what the reserve was built to already contain. The result is a valid n-coloring of the entire merged graph.
